---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How this site is built"
  description="The technical decisions behind nicholweb.ca, and why a marketing site doesn't need an application framework."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p>
        <h1>How this site is built</h1>
        <p class="muted">A short decision record. The source is public on GitHub.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The decision: Astro, not Next.js</h2>
        <p>
          Most of my recent client work is in Next.js, so it would have been the obvious choice. It's also the wrong
          one here. This site is a handful of pages and some case studies. Nothing on it is personalised, nothing changes
          per request, and there's no application state.
        </p>
        <p>
          For that, an application framework adds a JavaScript runtime and build complexity that visitors pay for and
          get nothing back from. <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> renders everything to static HTML at build time
          and ships no client-side JavaScript unless a page asks for it.
        </p>
        <h2>What that buys</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Static output.</strong> Every page is prebuilt HTML and CSS, served from Cloudflare's edge.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Content as files.</strong> Case studies are Markdown with a typed schema. Adding one is adding a file,
            and a missing field fails the build instead of breaking a page.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Type-checked builds.</strong> The build runs <code>astro check</code> first, so type errors never deploy.</li>
          <li><strong>Deploy on push.</strong> Cloudflare Pages builds and publishes every push to the main branch.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>What would change the decision</h2>
        <p>
          If the site needed per-visitor behaviour, authenticated areas or server-side logic beyond a form or an API
          endpoint, I'd reach for a full application framework. Until then, the simplest thing that works is the right
          architecture.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
